
3
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:15,720
Three, two, one. Go!

4
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:32,360
I won.

5
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:34,400
Let's do it again, please.

6
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:36,440
Okay.

7
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:39,480
I'll definitely win this time.

8
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:49,680
20 YEARS LATER

9
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:18,280
HONEY, CALL ME. I WANT TO TALK TO YOU

10
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:34,720
I WANT TO TALK TO YOU

11
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:37,400
Lucía, are you on your phone during class?

12
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:46,800
Ruth, honey, call me. Okay?

13
00:01:54,800 --> 00:01:57,080
<i>Latest news on Alejandro Huelva,</i>

14
00:01:57,160 --> 00:02:01,080
<i>the nine-year-old boy
who disappeared on October 23rd.</i>

15
00:02:01,160 --> 00:02:03,960
<i>Groups of volunteers,
mostly neighbors from Mieres,</i>

16
00:02:04,040 --> 00:02:06,400
<i>are collaborating with the Civil Guard</i>

17
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:10,040
<i>in a search that,
so far, has not been successful.</i>

18
00:02:10,120 --> 00:02:12,960
<i>The authorities have set up
a special phone number</i>

19
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:14,720
<i>that any citizen can call...</i>

20
00:02:15,320 --> 00:02:17,920
- Alejandro!
- Alejandro!

21
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,480
- Alejandro!
- Alejandro!

22
00:02:20,560 --> 00:02:23,600
<i>...about the case completely anonymously.</i>

23
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:29,080
<i>The number is 984 555 6704.</i>

24
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:31,160
<i>Now, on to other news.</i>

25
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:33,240
<i>The employment rate in the last quarter...</i>

26
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:18,000
THE TRUTHERS

27
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:03,200
Hey, all good?

28
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:24,440
MOM:
RUTH, PLEASE

29
00:04:24,520 --> 00:04:25,960
CALL ME. IT'S IMPORTANT

30
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:59,000
Large black coffee with milk, please.

31
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:13,640
- What is it?
- <i>Ruth...</i>

32
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:15,280
<i>It's Mom.</i>

33
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:17,600
- What?
- <i>Mom fell down.</i>

34
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:19,280
What? She what?

35
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:22,080
Where did she fall?

36
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:24,960
<i>Mom is dead, Ruth.</i>

37
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:03,720
<i>Last call for passengers
on flight 817 to Madrid.</i>

38
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:06,040
<i>Please proceed to the boarding gate.</i>

39
00:06:07,760 --> 00:06:09,240
URGENT
MISSING

40
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:18,240
<i>He went out on his bike
like every afternoon,</i>

41
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:20,640
<i>but hours went by,
and he didn't come back.</i>

42
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:22,800
<i>And... he still hasn't come back.</i>

43
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:26,160
<i>We know he's out there.
If someone sees him, please...</i>

44
00:06:26,240 --> 00:06:28,240
If that kid hasn't turned up yet...

45
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:32,200
What a tragedy. The town is in shock.

46
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:37,720
They go out looking for him every day,
but I doubt there's much hope.

47
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:08,760
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

48
00:07:19,120 --> 00:07:20,160
What happened?

49
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:26,520
We need to be strong.

50
00:07:29,400 --> 00:07:30,600
Me especially.

51
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:35,920
What's the Civil Guard doing here?

52
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:45,680
Okay. Thanks, Carlos.

53
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:50,000
Guys, I'm sorry, but...

54
00:07:50,080 --> 00:07:53,960
Even if it was an unfortunate accident,
we need to document what happened.

55
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:57,000
So what happened?

56
00:07:59,120 --> 00:08:00,960
She slipped in the shower.

57
00:08:01,480 --> 00:08:04,480
Apparently, she'd been drinking.

58
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:09,280
Impossible.

59
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:11,640
- Excuse me?
- No.

60
00:08:11,720 --> 00:08:14,480
She didn't... My mother didn't drink.

61
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:16,360
She'd been drinking lately.

62
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:20,600
She did.

63
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:24,600
Ruth, it seems that your mother,
at around midnight,

64
00:08:24,680 --> 00:08:27,320
went upstairs to the bathroom
to take a shower.

65
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:28,960
Shall we go upstairs?

66
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:30,040
Sure.

67
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:36,760
Your father was upstairs, in the attic,

68
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:39,960
and noticed that your mother
was taking a while to come out.

69
00:08:40,040 --> 00:08:43,960
I called her, and she didn't answer,
but I could hear the water running, so...

70
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:45,960
I had to break down the door.

71
00:08:49,960 --> 00:08:52,760
She was... motionless.

72
00:08:52,840 --> 00:08:54,480
She wasn't breathing.

73
00:08:54,560 --> 00:08:57,680
We believe that when she slipped,

74
00:08:57,760 --> 00:09:00,120
she fell onto the screen, and it broke.

75
00:09:00,720 --> 00:09:02,680
She hit her head on the floor and...

76
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:05,520
Well, she died quickly.

77
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:12,000
Sorry, you can't touch anything for now.

78
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:13,920
Sorry, it's just that...

79
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,440
My mother never locked the doors.

80
00:09:16,520 --> 00:09:19,520
She didn't like it.
She never let me lock them.

81
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:22,000
She'd freak out on me whenever I did.

82
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:22,920
Honey.

83
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:27,440
It's as if we're talking
about somebody else, isn't it?

84
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:29,760
Ruth.

85
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:31,640
I don't recognize her.

86
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:35,040
You haven't been here in almost two years.

87
00:09:36,200 --> 00:09:37,960
How could you recognize her?

88
00:09:46,760 --> 00:09:49,200
- Patience.
- Patience. We've got no other choice.

89
00:09:50,200 --> 00:09:51,960
I was just telling your father

90
00:09:52,040 --> 00:09:55,120
we still have paperwork,
routine stuff left.

91
00:09:55,200 --> 00:09:57,080
Well, you know.

92
00:09:57,160 --> 00:09:59,520
But as soon as we have
the toxicology report

93
00:09:59,600 --> 00:10:01,720
and confirm everything's in order,

94
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:06,080
the body will be made available
for the funeral, the viewing, and so on.

95
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:07,840
I hope it'll be as soon as possible.

96
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:10,640
In the meantime,
anything you need, I'm here, alright?

97
00:10:13,840 --> 00:10:15,160
Let's go.

98
00:10:15,240 --> 00:10:17,800
Martín, you know where to find me.

99
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:19,160
- Thanks again.
- No problem.

100
00:10:19,240 --> 00:10:21,240
- Good afternoon.
- Good afternoon.

101
00:10:44,680 --> 00:10:47,560
Well, if you need anything,
you know where I am.

102
00:10:50,600 --> 00:10:51,600
Dad.

103
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:57,520
Did something happen yesterday?

104
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:01,720
Why do you say that?

105
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:04,200
Because Mom called me.

106
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:06,720
Several times.

107
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:10,160
I was having a bad day and didn't answer.

108
00:11:10,240 --> 00:11:13,000
And she sent me messages too.

109
00:11:13,600 --> 00:11:14,600
What did she say?

110
00:11:17,680 --> 00:11:18,880
That she wanted to talk.

111
00:11:21,160 --> 00:11:22,640
We had a bit of a fight.

112
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:24,920
Why?

113
00:11:27,600 --> 00:11:29,720
Because I didn't like her drinking.

114
00:11:31,040 --> 00:11:33,680
I think that's why
she locked herself in the bathroom.

115
00:11:33,760 --> 00:11:36,920
So she could keep drinking
without me saying anything.

116
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:39,520
But her glass was downstairs.

117
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:40,680
What?

118
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:44,480
Her glass. She didn't take it with her.
She left it downstairs.

119
00:11:45,640 --> 00:11:47,840
She must have forgotten it. She was drunk.

120
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:49,960
What does it matter?

121
00:11:51,560 --> 00:11:53,200
It matters. It matters, Dad.

122
00:11:53,720 --> 00:11:57,520
She called me, and I didn't answer.
And I wanted to call her today.

123
00:11:57,600 --> 00:12:00,200
I was going to call her today, and now...

124
00:12:51,800 --> 00:12:55,280
<i>Ruth, I know you don't like
talking on the phone.</i>

125
00:12:56,080 --> 00:12:59,760
<i>But if I'm calling or texting you,
it's because it's important.</i>

126
00:12:59,840 --> 00:13:04,280
<i>Don't worry, I'm not going to bug you
about coming home anytime soon.</i>

127
00:13:04,360 --> 00:13:08,800
<i>I know flights are expensive and all that.</i>

128
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:12,360
<i>It's about your father, Ruth.</i>

129
00:13:12,440 --> 00:13:14,720
<i>Your father is not the man
you used to know.</i>

130
00:13:15,760 --> 00:13:18,160
<i>Your father is... He's a different person.</i>

131
00:13:18,240 --> 00:13:19,600
<i>For some time now.</i>

132
00:13:19,680 --> 00:13:22,640
<i>I didn't want to tell you
so you wouldn't worry.</i>

133
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:24,040
<i>But...</i>

134
00:13:25,960 --> 00:13:27,040
<i>Sorry.</i>

135
00:13:27,560 --> 00:13:30,760
<i>I thought I heard him coming.</i>

136
00:13:31,920 --> 00:13:32,920
<i>Ruth.</i>

137
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:35,320
<i>Your father...</i>

138
00:13:35,840 --> 00:13:37,560
<i>Your father is scaring me.</i>

139
00:13:38,080 --> 00:13:41,880
<i>He spends all day locked up in the attic.</i>

140
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:45,040
<i>I can't explain it to you now, but...</i>

141
00:13:46,040 --> 00:13:48,880
<i>I'm afraid that one day
he'll do something crazy.</i>

142
00:13:49,760 --> 00:13:51,680
<i>Call me, please.</i>

143
00:13:51,760 --> 00:13:52,800
<i>I love you.</i>

144
00:14:32,160 --> 00:14:35,640
{\an8}CIVIL GUARD
DO NOT ENTER

145
00:14:55,520 --> 00:14:56,520
Dad?

146
00:15:20,480 --> 00:15:21,880
Did you sleep well?

147
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:25,360
A bit runny.
That's how you like them, right?

148
00:15:28,360 --> 00:15:30,040
- There you go.
- Thank you.

149
00:15:32,360 --> 00:15:34,480
Do you want anything else? Some juice?

150
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:36,680
No, I'm not very hungry.

151
00:15:37,520 --> 00:15:39,560
Can I help you with anything today?

152
00:15:40,200 --> 00:15:41,600
No, don't worry.

153
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:45,160
We can't do much until the Civil Guard
gives us the go-ahead.

154
00:15:45,240 --> 00:15:46,840
I can do the shopping if you want.

155
00:15:47,640 --> 00:15:49,440
Or I can help you in the greenhouse.

156
00:15:49,520 --> 00:15:53,040
I've been neglecting
the greenhouse lately.

157
00:15:55,760 --> 00:15:57,040
Why's that?

158
00:15:57,120 --> 00:15:58,640
I've been busy.

159
00:16:00,400 --> 00:16:02,200
You're retired, Dad.

160
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:06,560
Do people stop being people
when they retire or what?

161
00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:08,320
Eat up.

162
00:16:13,040 --> 00:16:15,320
Last night I heard your mother's voice.

163
00:16:16,680 --> 00:16:18,000
In a dream or...?

164
00:16:18,080 --> 00:16:19,440
No.

165
00:16:19,520 --> 00:16:21,680
On your phone, I guess.

166
00:16:23,080 --> 00:16:24,520
Did she leave you a message?

167
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:29,480
No. They were old voice messages.

168
00:16:30,200 --> 00:16:31,400
Are you sure?

169
00:16:32,520 --> 00:16:33,440
Why?

170
00:16:34,200 --> 00:16:37,120
I'm your father, honey.
I can tell when you're lying to me.

171
00:16:37,760 --> 00:16:39,800
I wish you'd also notice when I drop hints

172
00:16:39,880 --> 00:16:41,960
because I don't want
to talk about something.

173
00:16:42,040 --> 00:16:44,000
Why won't you tell me
what she said to you?

174
00:16:44,080 --> 00:16:45,560
Because it was between us, Dad.

175
00:16:45,640 --> 00:16:47,440
Yeah, right. As usual.

176
00:16:47,920 --> 00:16:51,160
God forbid I get involved in your world.

177
00:16:51,760 --> 00:16:54,480
You were always like cats and dogs,
but if I stepped in,

178
00:16:54,560 --> 00:16:56,960
you'd close ranks
and there was no way to get through.

179
00:16:57,040 --> 00:16:59,080
It's not a big deal.

180
00:16:59,160 --> 00:17:01,200
Well, that's over now, honey.

181
00:17:02,560 --> 00:17:05,400
We can't have secrets anymore.

182
00:17:06,400 --> 00:17:08,960
Your mother is gone. Do you understand?

183
00:17:10,960 --> 00:17:11,960
Yes, Dad.

184
00:17:40,960 --> 00:17:42,520
Thank you very much.

185
00:17:42,600 --> 00:17:43,800
Ruth.

186
00:17:46,960 --> 00:17:49,520
The Civil Guard Sergeant called.

187
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:53,360
The coroner is done.
They're giving us Mom back.

188
00:17:54,320 --> 00:17:56,360
Did they find anything strange?

189
00:17:57,760 --> 00:17:59,800
What were they supposed to find?

190
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:02,200
She drank and fell down. That's it.

191
00:18:13,320 --> 00:18:14,320
Can I help you?

192
00:18:14,400 --> 00:18:16,440
No need, honey.

193
00:18:18,800 --> 00:18:20,200
Dad! Let me help you.

194
00:18:20,280 --> 00:18:22,320
I don't need your help.

195
00:18:22,400 --> 00:18:23,360
Give it to me.

196
00:18:24,480 --> 00:18:27,080
I'll race you whenever you want.
Let's see who wins.

197
00:18:27,160 --> 00:18:29,200
Better stick to laps, don't you think?

198
00:18:29,280 --> 00:18:30,840
That wouldn't be fair.

199
00:18:31,520 --> 00:18:34,920
No one can compete in the water
with a mermaid.

200
00:18:39,920 --> 00:18:42,560
This mermaid is a little out of practice.

201
00:18:46,520 --> 00:18:47,760
What are you doing?

202
00:18:48,280 --> 00:18:50,360
Throwing away this rubbish.

203
00:18:52,280 --> 00:18:53,800
Those are Mom's creams.

204
00:18:53,880 --> 00:18:55,160
Rubbish.

205
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:59,200
And those bastards
charge a fortune for them.

206
00:19:00,760 --> 00:19:03,200
You're swearing a lot lately, aren't you?

207
00:19:03,280 --> 00:19:09,080
Those sons of bitches ruin people's lives
and then take their money.

208
00:19:11,160 --> 00:19:12,520
Who are we talking about?

209
00:19:14,520 --> 00:19:16,640
Big Pharma.

210
00:19:17,640 --> 00:19:20,200
Do you know what they've done
in the United States?

211
00:19:20,280 --> 00:19:22,880
They have half the country
hooked on opioids.

212
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:25,000
Not to mention fentanyl.

213
00:19:25,080 --> 00:19:28,720
They drug them, ruin them,
and turn them into zombies.

214
00:19:31,080 --> 00:19:33,560
What does that have to do
with Mom's creams?

215
00:19:33,640 --> 00:19:35,120
You know how they make them?

216
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:37,240
They experiment on animals.

217
00:19:37,960 --> 00:19:38,960
I know.

218
00:19:39,480 --> 00:19:40,680
And on people.

219
00:19:42,440 --> 00:19:43,440
People?

220
00:19:43,520 --> 00:19:45,000
On children.

221
00:19:45,920 --> 00:19:47,560
Children like Alejandro.

222
00:19:47,640 --> 00:19:48,640
Haven't you heard?

223
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:51,880
Impossible not to hear about it.

224
00:19:53,360 --> 00:19:55,960
He has disappeared
from the face of the earth.

225
00:19:56,040 --> 00:19:58,880
And do you know
who's moved in here recently?

226
00:19:59,840 --> 00:20:02,640
The headquarters
of a big American pharmaceutical company.

227
00:20:02,720 --> 00:20:03,640
What a coincidence.

228
00:20:04,640 --> 00:20:06,920
There's no such thing
as coincidence, honey.

229
00:20:08,040 --> 00:20:09,920
I can send you links.

230
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:14,400
There are thousands of videos, articles,
people fighting to make this known.

231
00:20:14,480 --> 00:20:15,720
Dad.

232
00:20:16,400 --> 00:20:17,400
Okay.

233
00:20:18,280 --> 00:20:20,000
I don't know why I'm surprised.

234
00:20:20,880 --> 00:20:22,720
You're just like your mother.

235
00:20:30,400 --> 00:20:31,800
Hello, Sergeant. How are you?

236
00:20:31,880 --> 00:20:34,520
I'm Ruth, Martín's daughter.

237
00:20:34,600 --> 00:20:38,080
My father told me
that you have the coroner's report

238
00:20:38,160 --> 00:20:40,080
and that it says it was an accident.

239
00:20:40,160 --> 00:20:42,360
- <i>That's right.</i>
- Right.

240
00:20:42,440 --> 00:20:46,400
Well, I... Tell me if I'm out of line
or if I'm overstepping,

241
00:20:46,480 --> 00:20:51,640
but I'd like to know if it's possible...

242
00:20:51,720 --> 00:20:54,320
for me to see the report.

243
00:20:54,400 --> 00:20:55,640
Or...

244
00:20:56,160 --> 00:20:59,440
Or the photos or something because...

245
00:20:59,520 --> 00:21:03,720
Well, it's just that
I'd like to know exactly...

246
00:21:04,240 --> 00:21:05,640
what happened.

247
00:21:05,720 --> 00:21:06,720
And...

248
00:21:08,200 --> 00:21:09,200
Hello?

249
00:21:09,960 --> 00:21:12,880
<i>You want to see a copy of the report?</i>

250
00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:17,440
Yes, if it's possible. I'd like to.

251
00:21:18,840 --> 00:21:20,320
<i>No problem.</i>

252
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:21,400
Okay.

253
00:21:54,240 --> 00:21:57,200
WAKE UP

254
00:21:57,280 --> 00:21:59,120
<i>What exactly is that substance?</i>

255
00:21:59,200 --> 00:22:03,360
<i>Adrenochrome is obtained
by adrenalizing the blood of children.</i>

256
00:22:03,440 --> 00:22:05,640
<i>Children are abducted.</i>

257
00:22:05,720 --> 00:22:09,240
<i>Then they're terrorized, tortured,</i>

258
00:22:09,320 --> 00:22:12,240
<i>and subjected to atrocities
to saturate their blood with adrenaline.</i>

259
00:22:12,320 --> 00:22:15,320
<i>It's consumed as a drug
that rejuvenates the elites.</i>

260
00:22:15,400 --> 00:22:17,280
<i>Of course, those who can afford it.</i>

261
00:22:17,360 --> 00:22:20,440
<i>And supposedly,
it's mixed with another well-known drug.</i>

262
00:22:20,520 --> 00:22:22,440
<i>Indeed. Fentanyl.</i>

263
00:22:22,520 --> 00:22:25,120
<i>Fentanyl, which, by the way,
comes from Ukraine.</i>

264
00:22:25,200 --> 00:22:26,480
<i>What a coincidence.</i>

265
00:22:26,560 --> 00:22:27,880
<i>It's all connected.</i>

266
00:22:27,960 --> 00:22:30,960
<i>Why don't we have more information
about this terrifying subject?</i>

267
00:22:31,040 --> 00:22:33,520
<i>Because the media
doesn't want the truth to be known.</i>

268
00:22:33,600 --> 00:22:36,480
<i>Only a handful of us
actually know the truth.</i>

269
00:22:58,040 --> 00:22:59,080
I'm sorry.

270
00:23:06,640 --> 00:23:07,720
Hi, Grandma.

271
00:23:07,800 --> 00:23:09,040
Hello, dear.

272
00:23:11,720 --> 00:23:13,240
Hi, honey. How are you?

273
00:23:17,760 --> 00:23:19,120
How awful, dear.

274
00:23:20,280 --> 00:23:21,640
How's your father?

275
00:23:21,720 --> 00:23:24,840
Like all of us. In shock.

276
00:23:48,120 --> 00:23:49,520
Thank you.

277
00:23:50,520 --> 00:23:52,520
I haven't eaten anything for two days.

278
00:23:53,640 --> 00:23:56,320
I imagine your mother
lying on the floor and...

279
00:23:57,600 --> 00:24:00,400
I don't understand
how this could have happened.

280
00:24:01,920 --> 00:24:03,120
Well, I don't know.

281
00:24:04,480 --> 00:24:06,480
Did you know she was drinking?

282
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:08,920
Yes.

283
00:24:09,720 --> 00:24:11,680
Your mother hadn't been okay lately.

284
00:24:11,760 --> 00:24:15,560
She was feeling down. Sad.

285
00:24:16,840 --> 00:24:18,320
I didn't know anything.

286
00:24:19,440 --> 00:24:23,200
I don't know. It's so weird for me
to talk about this with you.

287
00:24:23,280 --> 00:24:24,840
Especially in the past tense.

288
00:24:27,400 --> 00:24:28,920
Was it because of my dad?

289
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:30,080
Look, your father...

290
00:24:30,160 --> 00:24:33,600
Don't mention your father,
because your father... is acting weird.

291
00:24:33,680 --> 00:24:36,040
Who knows. Retirement.

292
00:24:36,640 --> 00:24:38,600
It's likely not sitting well with him.

293
00:24:38,680 --> 00:24:40,400
And the pandemic.

294
00:24:40,480 --> 00:24:42,400
That was hard for him and for everyone.

295
00:24:42,480 --> 00:24:46,520
Did you know that we had to take
your mother to get vaccinated in secret?

296
00:24:47,520 --> 00:24:49,080
She was so worried.

297
00:24:49,160 --> 00:24:51,960
She was worried that he would find out.

298
00:24:52,040 --> 00:24:54,920
She didn't know
how he was going to take it. Bah.

299
00:24:57,920 --> 00:24:59,120
But you...?

300
00:25:01,320 --> 00:25:03,320
I don't know, do you think that...?

301
00:25:04,400 --> 00:25:07,000
Do you think they argued,
or he got violent or something?

302
00:25:07,080 --> 00:25:08,400
Maybe he...

303
00:25:08,480 --> 00:25:09,600
I don't really know.

304
00:25:09,680 --> 00:25:14,160
You know that your mother
didn't tell me much, or anyone else.

305
00:25:38,160 --> 00:25:39,240
Here you go.

306
00:25:39,320 --> 00:25:40,680
The copy of the report.

307
00:25:41,400 --> 00:25:44,080
There are some pictures
of how we found her on the floor.

308
00:25:44,160 --> 00:25:46,520
If I were you, I wouldn't look at them.

309
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,120
FORENSIC REPORT

310
00:26:08,840 --> 00:26:13,000
PROBABLE CAUSE OF DEATH:
SEVERE HEAD TRAUMA FROM ACCIDENTAL FALL

311
00:26:57,720 --> 00:26:59,880
Ruth, I'm so sorry.

312
00:27:02,240 --> 00:27:03,840
My deepest condolences.

313
00:27:04,960 --> 00:27:06,120
She worked us hard.

314
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:08,840
All day long doing laps, right?

315
00:27:08,920 --> 00:27:11,600
She was very strict, but easy to love.

316
00:27:12,520 --> 00:27:13,680
She missed you so much.

317
00:27:13,760 --> 00:27:15,040
Well.

318
00:27:15,720 --> 00:27:18,080
Sorry, do you know
who my father is talking to?

319
00:27:19,120 --> 00:27:21,360
That man? He's part of his search team.

320
00:27:23,000 --> 00:27:24,680
Search team for what?

321
00:27:25,200 --> 00:27:27,680
I don't know if you've heard,
but a child's missing.

322
00:27:27,760 --> 00:27:31,440
And the neighbors have organized
search teams to look for him.

323
00:27:31,520 --> 00:27:33,640
That's Mariano, the new commissioner.

324
00:27:33,720 --> 00:27:35,600
Luis, from the repair shop.

325
00:27:35,680 --> 00:27:38,560
And that's Alfonso, the police doctor.

326
00:27:39,240 --> 00:27:40,400
What?

327
00:27:41,360 --> 00:27:42,800
You know, the doctor...

328
00:27:43,880 --> 00:27:44,920
What's it called?

329
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:45,920
Coroner.

330
00:27:47,080 --> 00:27:48,040
The coroner.

331
00:27:54,240 --> 00:27:56,440
- Excuse me.
- Of course.

332
00:28:11,760 --> 00:28:12,880
"Alfonso."

333
00:28:12,960 --> 00:28:16,000
CORONER ALFONSO GARCÍA MARÍN

334
00:28:25,960 --> 00:28:28,600
Dear Lucía, may God watch over you.

335
00:28:29,320 --> 00:28:31,200
We are going to miss you.

336
00:28:31,280 --> 00:28:33,520
Especially your husband and your daughter.

337
00:28:34,560 --> 00:28:36,040
They are not alone.

338
00:28:36,120 --> 00:28:40,120
The good Lord will be with them
until they are reunited with you.

339
00:28:41,120 --> 00:28:42,960
Faith is so necessary

340
00:28:43,760 --> 00:28:45,480
at times like this.

341
00:28:46,200 --> 00:28:50,160
It's so important to believe
that someone is waiting for us...

342
00:28:50,240 --> 00:28:53,200
Dad, who's that woman
over there by the pillar?

343
00:28:54,840 --> 00:28:56,760
We only remember God...

344
00:28:56,840 --> 00:28:58,000
No idea.

345
00:28:58,920 --> 00:29:00,480
But we remember...

346
00:29:00,560 --> 00:29:03,200
She was also at home. At the wake.

347
00:29:03,280 --> 00:29:06,520
...how orphaned those who don't believe are.

348
00:29:06,600 --> 00:29:09,240
Or those who worship false idols.

349
00:29:10,360 --> 00:29:13,280
Or those who let themselves
be led astray by absurd beliefs

350
00:29:14,080 --> 00:29:17,120
in search of who knows what.

351
00:29:18,280 --> 00:29:21,200
Why do we have to put up
with so much bullshit?

352
00:29:22,160 --> 00:29:25,840
To false prophets,
the merchants of the temple,

353
00:29:25,920 --> 00:29:28,360
and the falsely enlightened ignorant,

354
00:29:28,440 --> 00:29:30,800
the Lord has words for them.

355
00:29:32,080 --> 00:29:33,520
I am the path.

356
00:29:34,480 --> 00:29:35,880
And the truth.

357
00:29:36,400 --> 00:29:37,640
And life itself.

358
00:30:32,320 --> 00:30:33,720
Arón!

359
00:30:34,480 --> 00:30:35,680
Arón?

360
00:30:41,200 --> 00:30:42,200
Ruth.

361
00:30:43,960 --> 00:30:45,280
It's been forever.

362
00:30:45,360 --> 00:30:46,520
Yeah.

363
00:30:52,480 --> 00:30:54,280
Hey, are you still dealing?

364
00:30:54,360 --> 00:30:57,080
I'll see if I can find something,
but only because it's you.

365
00:30:57,160 --> 00:30:58,800
I don't do this anymore.

366
00:30:58,880 --> 00:31:00,600
I've moved up in life.

367
00:31:00,680 --> 00:31:02,080
I'm a cleaner now.

368
00:31:04,160 --> 00:31:05,160
Okay.

369
00:31:08,600 --> 00:31:09,600
Here you go.

370
00:31:10,720 --> 00:31:12,120
Thanks.

371
00:31:12,200 --> 00:31:14,640
Sorry, I wanted to go to the funeral.

372
00:31:15,520 --> 00:31:19,680
But your mother had it in for me.
It didn't seem right.

373
00:31:19,760 --> 00:31:21,080
But now I regret it.

374
00:31:21,680 --> 00:31:23,600
You don't go to funerals for the dead,

375
00:31:23,680 --> 00:31:25,680
but for the living who stay behind.

376
00:31:26,280 --> 00:31:29,160
I was really grateful to those
who came to my father's funeral.

377
00:31:29,240 --> 00:31:30,680
Don't worry.

378
00:31:30,760 --> 00:31:33,360
It's not like my mother
was a big fan of yours.

379
00:31:34,800 --> 00:31:37,840
She needed someone to blame
for her daughter losing her way.

380
00:31:37,920 --> 00:31:41,120
What are you talking about? I'm not lost.

381
00:31:41,200 --> 00:31:43,800
But my life isn't going
in a straight line anymore.

382
00:31:43,880 --> 00:31:45,080
How beautiful.

383
00:31:46,040 --> 00:31:47,520
No, seriously.

384
00:31:47,600 --> 00:31:51,040
I get why your mom hated
the boyfriend who got you into weed.

385
00:31:51,120 --> 00:31:52,200
Boyfriend?

386
00:31:52,800 --> 00:31:55,560
Well, fuck buddy. Okay? Fuck buddy.

387
00:31:55,640 --> 00:31:57,720
I don't think she liked that either.

388
00:32:01,200 --> 00:32:03,960
But the one giving me
dirty looks lately is your dad.

389
00:32:04,680 --> 00:32:05,720
Why is that?

390
00:32:07,920 --> 00:32:11,400
I don't know. But he was less creepy
when he was a Civil Guard officer.

391
00:32:11,480 --> 00:32:14,600
When we met up, he'd even offer me
a security gig or something.

392
00:32:14,680 --> 00:32:16,520
Short shifts, good money.

393
00:32:17,320 --> 00:32:19,200
But since I started working at Certyx...

394
00:32:20,680 --> 00:32:22,360
Is that the new pharma company?

395
00:32:23,640 --> 00:32:26,880
It's not that he doesn't say hello.
He looks like he wants to kill me.

396
00:32:31,560 --> 00:32:32,560
What?

397
00:32:35,360 --> 00:32:36,400
I don't know.

398
00:32:36,480 --> 00:32:39,200
Maybe my father has a screw loose,

399
00:32:39,280 --> 00:32:41,960
but I'm the one who's going crazy now.

400
00:32:44,720 --> 00:32:46,520
He's acting weird, you know?

401
00:32:47,520 --> 00:32:48,720
Different.

402
00:32:50,000 --> 00:32:51,000
So?

403
00:32:53,400 --> 00:32:55,560
I've even wondered whether...

404
00:32:58,880 --> 00:33:01,240
I'm not the same as I was
a few years ago, either.

405
00:33:04,360 --> 00:33:05,320
You're the same.

406
00:33:10,600 --> 00:33:11,800
Damn.

407
00:33:13,960 --> 00:33:14,960
How come?

408
00:33:17,520 --> 00:33:19,520
Colon cancer survivor.

409
00:33:19,600 --> 00:33:20,680
How about that?

410
00:33:23,760 --> 00:33:25,560
I don't know. I had no idea.

411
00:33:26,840 --> 00:33:29,760
Yeah, well, we haven't talked in a while.

412
00:33:29,840 --> 00:33:32,040
I wasn't going to tell you in an email.

413
00:33:32,640 --> 00:33:37,040
It's true that this little bag
has changed my life a bit, but oh well.

414
00:33:39,360 --> 00:33:42,240
Some days it feels like
the most fucked-up thing in the world.

415
00:33:42,320 --> 00:33:44,160
And other days, I'm thankful for it.

416
00:33:45,200 --> 00:33:47,840
Because it's proof
that I came close to dying, and I didn't.

417
00:33:48,520 --> 00:33:51,000
That always makes me want to celebrate.

418
00:33:53,240 --> 00:33:54,240
Oh really?

419
00:34:25,040 --> 00:34:26,040
What?

420
00:34:27,360 --> 00:34:30,880
It's just that right now, with the bag on...

421
00:34:32,360 --> 00:34:33,800
What? Can't you do it?

422
00:34:33,880 --> 00:34:37,040
I don't know. I really don't know.

423
00:34:38,680 --> 00:34:40,960
I haven't hooked up with anyone
since I've had it.

424
00:34:41,040 --> 00:34:42,560
And why is that?

425
00:34:42,640 --> 00:34:46,240
It doesn't make me feel very attractive.

426
00:34:47,000 --> 00:34:48,000
To be honest.

427
00:34:48,800 --> 00:34:50,840
You're hotter than ever.

428
00:34:50,920 --> 00:34:52,000
Really?

429
00:35:09,560 --> 00:35:11,240
Look at this huge building.

430
00:35:12,400 --> 00:35:13,680
This is where I work.

431
00:35:24,280 --> 00:35:26,040
It's been a fun night.

432
00:35:31,000 --> 00:35:33,040
- Take care.
- You too.

433
00:36:17,000 --> 00:36:18,280
What are you doing?

434
00:36:19,840 --> 00:36:21,040
Can't you see?

435
00:36:23,120 --> 00:36:24,560
So soon?

436
00:36:25,400 --> 00:36:28,800
The sooner it's done,
the sooner we can get on with our lives.

437
00:36:30,400 --> 00:36:32,400
The sooner
you can get into the attic, right?

438
00:36:42,280 --> 00:36:44,160
What do you do up there, Dad?

439
00:36:47,520 --> 00:36:51,000
I read. I watch videos. I inform myself.

440
00:36:52,360 --> 00:36:54,360
That's what everyone should do.

441
00:36:55,960 --> 00:36:58,040
And you need to lock yourself in for that?

442
00:36:58,120 --> 00:37:00,760
I don't like people
meddling in my business.

443
00:37:00,840 --> 00:37:02,480
You were the same.

444
00:37:02,560 --> 00:37:05,680
You made us put locks
all over the fucking house.

445
00:37:05,760 --> 00:37:07,320
The "fucking house"?

446
00:37:07,400 --> 00:37:08,720
Yes.

447
00:37:08,800 --> 00:37:11,800
I was a fucking teenager, Dad. Unlike you.

448
00:37:11,880 --> 00:37:14,280
You've never talked to me
like that before.

449
00:37:16,800 --> 00:37:19,360
Maybe that's what you needed, honey.

450
00:37:19,960 --> 00:37:21,280
A few hard truths.

451
00:37:22,920 --> 00:37:24,200
I don't recognize you.

452
00:37:26,680 --> 00:37:28,560
But you're still the same.

453
00:37:29,320 --> 00:37:32,280
You even had to leave
your mother's funeral halfway through.

454
00:37:35,120 --> 00:37:36,120
Thank you.

455
00:37:56,040 --> 00:38:00,200
OXIDATION PROCESS
ADRENALINE ⇒ ADRENOCHROME

456
00:38:19,200 --> 00:38:24,000
Ruth, I thought maybe
you'd like to keep Mom's phone.

457
00:38:24,080 --> 00:38:25,320
It's almost new.

458
00:38:25,400 --> 00:38:27,440
And yours clearly isn't.

459
00:38:29,600 --> 00:38:30,600
Thank you.

460
00:38:32,120 --> 00:38:34,040
Damn, this one is expensive.

461
00:38:34,120 --> 00:38:34,960
Yes.

462
00:38:36,080 --> 00:38:38,600
That really bothered her.

463
00:38:39,120 --> 00:38:41,760
She asked me
why I had spent so much money.

464
00:38:42,520 --> 00:38:43,920
Everything bothered her.

465
00:38:44,760 --> 00:38:45,960
Since when?

466
00:38:46,720 --> 00:38:49,560
At first, she was angry with you,
because you had left.

467
00:38:49,640 --> 00:38:51,960
Because you hadn't finished your studies.

468
00:38:52,040 --> 00:38:54,280
Because you were out there,
doing God knows what.

469
00:38:54,360 --> 00:38:58,160
But what's the point of being angry
with someone who's not there?

470
00:38:59,640 --> 00:39:02,120
The only person
she had in front of her was me.

471
00:39:02,200 --> 00:39:05,360
The only person to blame for everything.

472
00:39:05,440 --> 00:39:08,520
She blamed me
for not being stricter with you.

473
00:39:09,400 --> 00:39:12,640
It's always easier to blame someone else.

474
00:39:15,800 --> 00:39:17,520
It was a very hard time.

475
00:39:19,080 --> 00:39:20,760
But don't worry.

476
00:39:20,840 --> 00:39:22,680
I'm much better now.

477
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:25,200
I'm leaving.

478
00:39:27,640 --> 00:39:28,520
Dad.

479
00:39:30,080 --> 00:39:32,960
Do you have the phone's password?

480
00:39:33,040 --> 00:39:36,560
You don't need it.
I did a factory reset yesterday.

481
00:39:39,200 --> 00:39:40,960
And everything that was on it?

482
00:39:41,880 --> 00:39:43,480
Your mother is dead.

483
00:39:43,560 --> 00:39:46,640
Why would she need what was on it?

484
00:39:46,720 --> 00:39:48,040
Why would you need it?

485
00:39:48,640 --> 00:39:50,000
Me?

486
00:39:50,080 --> 00:39:51,120
I don't.

487
00:39:51,200 --> 00:39:52,960
- Never mind.
- Sure.

488
00:41:32,680 --> 00:41:35,920
Do you think you can find anything
that might have been inside?

489
00:41:36,000 --> 00:41:38,440
I can try to recover
whatever was in the cloud.

490
00:41:38,520 --> 00:41:40,840
But the shop in town
will do it better, for sure.

491
00:41:40,920 --> 00:41:43,120
In the town everyone knows my father.

492
00:41:44,040 --> 00:41:45,640
So what?

493
00:41:48,000 --> 00:41:49,480
Ruth, what the hell is going on?

494
00:41:54,240 --> 00:41:56,760
<i>He spends all day locked up in the attic.</i>

495
00:41:57,920 --> 00:42:00,560
<i>I can't explain it to you now, but...</i>

496
00:42:00,640 --> 00:42:03,360
<i>I'm afraid that one day
he'll do something crazy.</i>

497
00:42:05,920 --> 00:42:10,440
Something crazy at your father's age
could be anything, Ruth. Anything.

498
00:42:11,880 --> 00:42:13,680
A divorce. Or buying a convertible.

499
00:42:13,760 --> 00:42:15,720
But she said she's afraid, Arón.

500
00:42:16,320 --> 00:42:18,560
My mother wasn't afraid of anything.

501
00:42:19,360 --> 00:42:20,440
Yeah.

502
00:42:20,960 --> 00:42:22,960
But you hadn't seen her in a while.

503
00:42:23,040 --> 00:42:25,200
Damn, I know. But she's my mother.

504
00:42:25,280 --> 00:42:27,440
She used to go to the bathroom
with the door open.

505
00:42:28,080 --> 00:42:30,960
She didn't like closed doors
and she didn't like secrets.

506
00:42:31,040 --> 00:42:34,440
And I'm sure she didn't like my father
locking himself in the attic.

507
00:42:36,800 --> 00:42:39,400
What if she went in there
and saw something?

508
00:42:39,480 --> 00:42:41,920
Something my father
doesn't want anyone to see.

509
00:42:42,000 --> 00:42:44,000
And they argued
and that's why she called me.

510
00:42:45,040 --> 00:42:46,560
And that's why she got killed.

511
00:42:48,000 --> 00:42:50,920
Hang on, Ruth. Let's see.

512
00:42:51,000 --> 00:42:52,680
Have you talked to the Civil Guard?

513
00:42:52,760 --> 00:42:55,280
All the civil guards
are friends of my father.

514
00:42:55,360 --> 00:42:56,560
I mean, I don't know.

515
00:42:56,640 --> 00:42:58,200
Do you think I'm imagining things?

516
00:42:58,280 --> 00:43:02,000
No, I don't know. I don't know, Ruth.

517
00:43:04,920 --> 00:43:07,120
Is that why you're staying
a few more days?

518
00:43:11,680 --> 00:43:15,080
I need to know
that what I'm imagining is not true.

519
00:43:16,080 --> 00:43:18,240
- We're in.
- What's in there?

520
00:43:18,320 --> 00:43:20,480
It says there are 200 photos.

521
00:43:21,160 --> 00:43:23,120
That doesn't seem like a lot to me.

522
00:43:23,200 --> 00:43:25,200
Yeah, it's a new phone.

523
00:43:25,720 --> 00:43:27,680
I think she got it for Christmas.

524
00:43:28,200 --> 00:43:30,240
Nothing. There's nothing here.

525
00:43:30,320 --> 00:43:32,680
Couldn't there be a folder?

526
00:43:33,280 --> 00:43:35,520
Something he didn't want you to see?

527
00:43:35,600 --> 00:43:36,600
What's that?

528
00:43:36,680 --> 00:43:39,320
There's one. "Untitled."

529
00:43:40,080 --> 00:43:41,280
Close your eyes.

530
00:43:41,360 --> 00:43:42,400
Why?

531
00:43:42,480 --> 00:43:44,560
- Just in case.
- Just in case what?

532
00:43:44,640 --> 00:43:47,800
Imagine there are nudes
of your mother or your father--

533
00:43:47,880 --> 00:43:48,920
Come on, Arón.

534
00:43:49,000 --> 00:43:51,640
Or both of them naked.
You don't want to see that.

535
00:43:51,720 --> 00:43:54,120
- You're so dumb.
- And neither do I, honestly.

536
00:43:54,200 --> 00:43:58,120
But if that's what I have to do
to marry you, I'll do it.

537
00:44:00,400 --> 00:44:02,920
I'm kidding. Come on. Close your eyes.

538
00:44:04,200 --> 00:44:06,160
Okay. Go ahead.

539
00:44:07,440 --> 00:44:08,440
Can I open them?

540
00:44:09,360 --> 00:44:10,760
Who's this?

541
00:44:12,480 --> 00:44:13,720
Fuck.

542
00:44:15,000 --> 00:44:16,720
Fuck. I knew it.

543
00:44:16,800 --> 00:44:19,120
- I knew it.
- What's going on?

544
00:44:19,200 --> 00:44:21,560
It's that woman that was both
at the wake and the funeral.

545
00:44:21,640 --> 00:44:25,880
I asked my father who she was,
and he said he didn't know.

546
00:44:25,960 --> 00:44:27,680
Who the fuck is she?

547
00:44:27,760 --> 00:44:29,120
I don't know.

548
00:44:29,200 --> 00:44:31,720
Only your mother and your father know.

549
00:44:31,800 --> 00:44:34,200
Well, I want to know too.
I'm going to find out.

550
00:44:34,280 --> 00:44:35,760
How are you going to find that out?

551
00:44:35,840 --> 00:44:40,200
I'll show the pictures to my father
and make him tell me the fucking truth.

552
00:44:40,280 --> 00:44:41,720
And you'll do that on your own?

553
00:44:51,160 --> 00:44:53,680
Shit, Arón.
You were blazing in the van, seriously?

554
00:44:53,760 --> 00:44:56,560
I was nervous. But I can handle it, okay?

555
00:45:00,160 --> 00:45:01,440
Hi, Dad.

556
00:45:02,080 --> 00:45:03,520
Do you remember Arón?

557
00:45:08,360 --> 00:45:09,800
I invited him to dinner.

558
00:45:10,680 --> 00:45:11,520
Good evening.

559
00:45:13,160 --> 00:45:14,280
Great.

560
00:45:17,120 --> 00:45:19,720
Sorry, I haven't been cooking much lately.

561
00:45:20,760 --> 00:45:22,920
I just make quick meals.

562
00:45:23,000 --> 00:45:25,560
Don't worry, Martín.
This is Michelin-star level for me.

563
00:45:25,640 --> 00:45:27,400
Do you eat a lot of junk?

564
00:45:27,920 --> 00:45:31,120
In the morning I clean shit,
and at night I eat it.

565
00:45:32,480 --> 00:45:34,360
Maybe that's where the cancer came from.

566
00:45:34,440 --> 00:45:35,520
Dad.

567
00:45:35,600 --> 00:45:38,480
No, it's not your fault.

568
00:45:38,560 --> 00:45:41,920
It's the chemicals
they put in everything nowadays.

569
00:45:42,440 --> 00:45:44,240
And they do it knowingly.

570
00:45:44,320 --> 00:45:47,640
They make you sick,
and then sell you the cure.

571
00:45:48,320 --> 00:45:50,200
Meanwhile, they ruin public healthcare,

572
00:45:50,280 --> 00:45:52,120
so we all have to pay for it.

573
00:45:54,880 --> 00:45:55,920
"They"?

574
00:45:56,880 --> 00:45:58,200
The people you work for.

575
00:45:59,120 --> 00:46:00,560
Martín, I mop floors.

576
00:46:01,800 --> 00:46:03,200
You work for murderers.

577
00:46:03,800 --> 00:46:05,840
And kidnappers.

578
00:46:05,920 --> 00:46:06,880
Don't forget that.

579
00:46:07,840 --> 00:46:09,520
Do you think this is funny?

580
00:46:09,600 --> 00:46:11,520
Wait, Martín, let me get this straight.

581
00:46:12,640 --> 00:46:15,520
You think my bosses
are to blame for my cancer?

582
00:46:15,600 --> 00:46:18,240
I don't know if your bosses
are specifically to blame, but...

583
00:46:18,760 --> 00:46:20,880
Well, you mean the big companies.

584
00:46:20,960 --> 00:46:22,560
And the governments.

585
00:46:24,240 --> 00:46:25,480
Martín, come on.

586
00:46:25,560 --> 00:46:27,520
Who else is to blame?

587
00:46:27,600 --> 00:46:30,760
Well, it's always easier
to blame someone else.

588
00:46:31,480 --> 00:46:33,320
I wish someone was to blame.

589
00:46:33,400 --> 00:46:36,520
I wish cancer was created somewhere,

590
00:46:36,600 --> 00:46:38,760
and we could all go there,
destroy everything

591
00:46:38,840 --> 00:46:40,760
and get rid of that shit.

592
00:46:40,840 --> 00:46:42,720
But that's not how it goes, Martín.

593
00:46:42,800 --> 00:46:44,080
- Oh, really?
- No.

594
00:46:44,160 --> 00:46:45,360
How does it go, then?

595
00:46:45,440 --> 00:46:47,040
You got it? Tough shit.

596
00:46:47,120 --> 00:46:49,320
And you never know
when it's going to come back.

597
00:46:49,920 --> 00:46:52,000
So you take all that fear,
and you swallow it.

598
00:46:52,960 --> 00:46:55,000
But you don't swallow it.
You light it up, right?

599
00:47:00,600 --> 00:47:04,520
You toke, lie on the couch,
and you do nothing.

600
00:47:04,600 --> 00:47:06,840
And let them do whatever they want.

601
00:47:06,920 --> 00:47:08,800
And what do you do, Dad?

602
00:47:08,880 --> 00:47:12,080
I ask myself questions. I investigate.

603
00:47:12,160 --> 00:47:13,200
I go further.

604
00:47:15,600 --> 00:47:17,360
That's what you do with your friend?

605
00:47:17,440 --> 00:47:20,360
The one at the wake
you said you didn't know?

606
00:47:22,040 --> 00:47:25,360
You see, Dad,
Mom had pictures of you two on her phone.

607
00:47:26,360 --> 00:47:28,080
Walking around town.

608
00:47:28,600 --> 00:47:29,800
What are you doing, honey?

609
00:47:29,880 --> 00:47:32,320
Nothing. I'm investigating. Just like you.

610
00:47:33,040 --> 00:47:34,040
Who is she?

611
00:47:36,040 --> 00:47:37,480
Her name is Berta.

612
00:47:39,000 --> 00:47:40,320
Her name is Berta.

613
00:47:40,800 --> 00:47:41,640
Great.

614
00:47:43,560 --> 00:47:45,840
- Who is Berta?
- Berta is a friend.

615
00:47:46,840 --> 00:47:47,680
A friend.

616
00:47:47,760 --> 00:47:51,320
Yes, at my age,
one can also make new friends.

617
00:47:51,400 --> 00:47:55,240
But it's easy for people in town
to get the wrong idea.

618
00:47:55,320 --> 00:47:57,200
That's why I prefer to keep quiet.

619
00:47:57,800 --> 00:47:59,640
- And where?
- What?

620
00:47:59,720 --> 00:48:01,480
Where? Where did you meet her?

621
00:48:03,000 --> 00:48:04,200
On the internet.

622
00:48:04,280 --> 00:48:05,160
On Tinder?

623
00:48:06,600 --> 00:48:09,560
No. On research forums.

624
00:48:09,640 --> 00:48:11,040
Who goes on those forums, Dad?

625
00:48:11,120 --> 00:48:13,680
A lot more people than you think.

626
00:48:13,760 --> 00:48:15,600
- Oh, really?
- Yes.

627
00:48:15,680 --> 00:48:18,280
Most people keep quiet because...

628
00:48:19,240 --> 00:48:20,320
Why?

629
00:48:21,360 --> 00:48:24,280
Because it's not easy
to talk about certain things.

630
00:48:25,440 --> 00:48:27,920
Because you can lose people along the way.

631
00:48:29,560 --> 00:48:31,360
Is that what happened to you?

632
00:48:32,880 --> 00:48:34,120
With Mom?

633
00:48:36,320 --> 00:48:37,240
It could be.

634
00:48:40,760 --> 00:48:42,880
Berta has lost a lot of people.

635
00:48:43,400 --> 00:48:46,680
She lost her husband.
She lost custody of her children.

636
00:48:47,600 --> 00:48:49,600
And right now she feels very lonely.

637
00:48:49,680 --> 00:48:53,320
As a friend, I try to keep her company,
spend time with her.

638
00:48:53,400 --> 00:48:55,320
I'm trying to help a friend.

639
00:48:55,840 --> 00:48:57,560
Is that wrong?

640
00:49:11,120 --> 00:49:12,160
What do you think?

641
00:49:14,000 --> 00:49:16,640
I understand why you're like this, really.

642
00:49:16,720 --> 00:49:22,720
But regardless of everything going on
in his head, he's still the same person.

643
00:49:23,240 --> 00:49:26,560
I mean, he's a civil guard,
but he's a good guy.

644
00:49:26,640 --> 00:49:28,480
Even in that story he's got in his head,

645
00:49:28,560 --> 00:49:31,800
he's cast himself as the hero
saving kids from the evil elite.

646
00:49:39,640 --> 00:49:43,000
Hey, this room brings back memories.

647
00:49:44,440 --> 00:49:47,280
It'd be exciting doing it here,
with your dad next door.

648
00:49:47,360 --> 00:49:49,440
- Like in the old days.
- Oh, Arón.

649
00:49:57,680 --> 00:49:59,600
Do you know how to force open a door?

650
00:50:01,840 --> 00:50:05,120
I need to get into the attic
and see what my mother saw.

651
00:50:07,440 --> 00:50:08,520
And why should I care?

652
00:50:09,680 --> 00:50:13,360
I don't know anyone else
who can break a lock.

653
00:50:14,280 --> 00:50:15,440
And I can?

654
00:50:15,960 --> 00:50:18,240
Why? Because I used to sell weed?

655
00:50:18,320 --> 00:50:22,040
I don't know. I was just wondering
if you could help me.

656
00:50:23,680 --> 00:50:25,520
Ruth, I'm not a criminal.

657
00:50:29,280 --> 00:50:33,640
I know, Arón. I thought
maybe you might know someone.

658
00:50:33,720 --> 00:50:36,480
Who do you think I hang out with?

659
00:50:37,000 --> 00:50:38,040
Who do you think I am?

660
00:50:41,480 --> 00:50:43,480
Forget it, Ruth. It doesn't matter.

661
00:51:19,480 --> 00:51:23,440
<i>First, we take out the cylinder carefully.</i>

662
00:51:23,520 --> 00:51:26,800
<i>Like this. Make sure the clips don't snag...</i>

663
00:53:16,720 --> 00:53:17,640
<i>Hello?</i>

664
00:53:17,720 --> 00:53:20,520
I'm sorry, Arón. I was...

665
00:53:20,600 --> 00:53:23,160
<i>Hey, have you seen my badge over there?</i>

666
00:53:23,240 --> 00:53:24,120
Your what?

667
00:53:24,720 --> 00:53:27,080
<i>It has my picture and a barcode.</i>

668
00:53:27,600 --> 00:53:30,120
<i>And a blue ribbon.
I can't find it anywhere.</i>

669
00:53:30,200 --> 00:53:31,440
Where did you leave it?

670
00:53:31,520 --> 00:53:33,240
<i>I always leave it hanging in the van.</i>

671
00:53:33,320 --> 00:53:35,880
<i>But maybe I accidentally left it
at your place yesterday.</i>

672
00:53:35,960 --> 00:53:39,040
Does that badge let just anyone
into the pharmaceutical company?

673
00:53:39,120 --> 00:53:40,360
<i>Yes, of course.</i>

674
00:53:41,920 --> 00:53:44,280
- I see.
- <i>Ruth, what are you thinking?</i>

675
00:53:44,360 --> 00:53:46,320
- It's weird, isn't it?
- <i>What do you mean?</i>

676
00:53:46,400 --> 00:53:50,440
Don't you think it's weird
that you come here and lose your badge?

677
00:53:50,520 --> 00:53:53,000
- Did you leave the van unlocked?
- <i>It must be somewhere.</i>

678
00:53:53,080 --> 00:53:56,360
<i>Don't worry.
I'll see if I can get a duplicate. Bye.</i>

679
00:54:13,800 --> 00:54:16,840
LAS VEGAS
PUBLIC SCHOOL

680
00:54:23,880 --> 00:54:25,880
My ex-wife is not well.

681
00:54:27,760 --> 00:54:31,200
I had to get a restraining order.
And they granted it.

682
00:54:31,960 --> 00:54:33,120
Without hesitation.

683
00:54:33,640 --> 00:54:35,160
Because she's dangerous.

684
00:54:35,960 --> 00:54:37,160
She's dangerous.

685
00:54:37,960 --> 00:54:39,600
In what way?

686
00:54:39,680 --> 00:54:41,200
Well, she lost her mind.

687
00:54:41,280 --> 00:54:42,480
She was...

688
00:54:43,640 --> 00:54:46,600
She was a wonderful woman.
She was great. She was...

689
00:54:47,880 --> 00:54:50,080
But she lost it.

690
00:54:50,720 --> 00:54:51,560
She got sick.

691
00:54:51,640 --> 00:54:56,960
She went through a severe depression
that lasted for many months.

692
00:54:57,040 --> 00:54:58,240
It was terrible.

693
00:54:59,040 --> 00:55:00,920
And she locked herself up.

694
00:55:01,000 --> 00:55:03,960
She isolated herself
and started to believe in nonsense.

695
00:55:04,040 --> 00:55:06,480
She said that birds were not birds.

696
00:55:07,160 --> 00:55:08,960
That they were watching us.

697
00:55:09,040 --> 00:55:13,600
That planes were spraying
nanotechnology particles to control us.

698
00:55:13,680 --> 00:55:16,800
Suddenly, some really bad guys
were controlling everything.

699
00:55:17,520 --> 00:55:21,880
And she had to defeat them.
Then all the pain in the world would end.

700
00:55:23,200 --> 00:55:26,000
That's when she became obsessed
with the disappearances.

701
00:55:26,760 --> 00:55:28,480
With the missing children.

702
00:55:28,560 --> 00:55:31,640
There was no way to convince her
that it was all nonsense?

703
00:55:31,720 --> 00:55:32,680
I wish.

704
00:55:32,760 --> 00:55:36,080
She started obsessively
watching my kids. Our kids.

705
00:55:36,720 --> 00:55:40,840
When they came home,
she'd search them for cameras.

706
00:55:40,920 --> 00:55:44,760
She was on that damn control all day.

707
00:55:46,960 --> 00:55:49,760
She renewed her gun license
and had them all over the house.

708
00:55:49,840 --> 00:55:54,280
And, instead of stopping there,
it just got worse and worse.

709
00:55:54,360 --> 00:55:58,040
My ex-wife, when she was in the military,
was an expert on munitions.

710
00:55:59,520 --> 00:56:03,760
It was like she might blow
something up at any moment.

711
00:56:03,840 --> 00:56:05,720
I know that...

712
00:56:06,600 --> 00:56:09,680
One day I will see her name
in the newspapers. I know it.

713
00:56:11,840 --> 00:56:14,160
My father is like a different person.

714
00:56:14,880 --> 00:56:17,320
I know what I'm going to tell you is hard.

715
00:56:17,840 --> 00:56:19,040
But get away from him.

716
00:56:19,120 --> 00:56:22,280
Stay away, as far as you can.

717
00:57:03,720 --> 00:57:05,000
What are you doing?

718
00:57:06,720 --> 00:57:07,920
Practicing.

719
00:57:08,600 --> 00:57:09,560
Is there a problem?

720
00:57:12,520 --> 00:57:14,320
I've never seen you shoot before.

721
00:57:22,640 --> 00:57:25,560
What about the gun? Don't you
have to give it back when you retire?

722
00:57:26,920 --> 00:57:29,400
You never know
when you're going to need it.

723
00:59:33,920 --> 00:59:35,600
Good night, Ruth.

724
01:00:10,320 --> 01:00:12,640
Who were you talking to? Is it bad news?

725
01:00:12,720 --> 01:00:15,600
No. Just my employer in Berlin.

726
01:00:20,000 --> 01:00:20,920
Where are you going?

727
01:00:22,000 --> 01:00:24,600
We're still looking for Alejandro.

728
01:00:25,760 --> 01:00:28,600
I'm going to the lake.
You want to come along?

729
01:00:28,680 --> 01:00:30,240
You loved it when you were little.

730
01:00:36,000 --> 01:00:37,520
Okay.

731
01:00:44,320 --> 01:00:48,320
We have combed the entire forest
and we have found nothing.

732
01:00:48,400 --> 01:00:50,520
If he had gotten lost, if he had fallen,

733
01:00:50,600 --> 01:00:54,080
or if he had died,
we would have found the body.

734
01:00:56,760 --> 01:00:58,560
So, what are we doing here?

735
01:00:58,640 --> 01:01:00,960
- There are tunnels.
- Tunnels?

736
01:01:01,040 --> 01:01:03,400
Yes. They're hundreds of years old.

737
01:01:03,480 --> 01:01:08,040
During the war, the Maquis used them
to go see their families in the town.

738
01:01:08,120 --> 01:01:09,360
Look.

739
01:01:11,040 --> 01:01:12,040
See?

740
01:01:12,720 --> 01:01:15,320
Do you know what's on the other side now?

741
01:01:15,400 --> 01:01:17,720
- What?
- The pharma company.

742
01:01:21,120 --> 01:01:22,480
Right.

743
01:01:22,560 --> 01:01:23,640
Shall we?

744
01:01:34,520 --> 01:01:38,520
NO SWIMMING

745
01:01:51,280 --> 01:01:52,840
Enough already. Okay, Dad?

746
01:01:54,520 --> 01:01:57,520
What if the entrance to the tunnels
was in the lake?

747
01:01:58,560 --> 01:02:00,840
They said there were spills
a few years ago.

748
01:02:00,920 --> 01:02:03,120
That's why no one goes swimming.

749
01:02:03,640 --> 01:02:05,280
People use it as a dump.

750
01:02:05,360 --> 01:02:09,080
But what if it's a cover-up
to keep people out of the water,

751
01:02:09,160 --> 01:02:10,600
so they don't find the tunnels?

752
01:02:13,000 --> 01:02:15,480
I'll have to come back with a wetsuit.

753
01:02:15,560 --> 01:02:16,560
You know what?

754
01:02:17,080 --> 01:02:18,560
It won't be necessary.

755
01:02:20,200 --> 01:02:21,080
What are you doing?

756
01:02:22,200 --> 01:02:24,480
- Looking for the tunnels.
- What?

757
01:02:25,760 --> 01:02:27,760
It's full of garbage!

758
01:02:29,880 --> 01:02:31,520
Where are the tunnels, Dad?

759
01:02:31,600 --> 01:02:34,080
This way? Or that way?

760
01:02:36,800 --> 01:02:38,560
What are you doing?

761
01:02:38,640 --> 01:02:40,160
The same as you.

762
01:02:40,240 --> 01:02:42,720
The same crazy things you do every day.

763
01:02:42,800 --> 01:02:44,280
It's not funny, is it?

764
01:02:53,840 --> 01:02:55,520
I don't see anything.

765
01:02:57,240 --> 01:02:58,840
Maybe this way?

766
01:03:01,960 --> 01:03:03,000
What's wrong?

767
01:03:05,200 --> 01:03:06,240
Fuck.

768
01:03:10,160 --> 01:03:12,320
- I cut myself.
- I told you.

769
01:03:28,800 --> 01:03:29,800
Take me home.

770
01:03:31,000 --> 01:03:32,040
What?

771
01:03:32,120 --> 01:03:34,360
Do you know how much crap is down there?

772
01:03:34,440 --> 01:03:37,080
I know. In fact, I warned you.

773
01:03:37,160 --> 01:03:39,320
I need to clean the wound
or it'll get infected.

774
01:03:39,400 --> 01:03:40,760
It's nothing.

775
01:03:40,840 --> 01:03:42,720
I want to check something up there.

776
01:03:42,800 --> 01:03:44,520
Hurry up. It's going to rain.

777
01:03:57,320 --> 01:04:01,480
It's like the only thing you care about
is your fucking obsession with that kid.

778
01:04:01,560 --> 01:04:05,040
We just buried Mom,
and you're at it again, dammit.

779
01:04:05,120 --> 01:04:06,800
I don't know, I feel like...

780
01:04:06,880 --> 01:04:08,560
Like Mom's thing pissed you off

781
01:04:08,640 --> 01:04:10,800
because it interrupted
your search for two days.

782
01:04:10,880 --> 01:04:13,720
Are you telling me that I don't care
about my wife's death?

783
01:04:14,320 --> 01:04:16,640
Why do you care so much about that kid?

784
01:04:17,160 --> 01:04:19,040
What's up? Do you want to impress her?

785
01:04:19,120 --> 01:04:20,120
Who?

786
01:04:21,280 --> 01:04:22,600
Your friend, Berta.

787
01:04:22,680 --> 01:04:25,040
Is she the one who brainwashed you?

788
01:04:25,560 --> 01:04:28,160
I don't know. Tell me.
Are you in love or something?

789
01:04:28,680 --> 01:04:31,400
I've only ever loved one woman,
your mother.

790
01:04:31,480 --> 01:04:32,720
Despite the drinking.

791
01:04:32,800 --> 01:04:37,280
Despite the fact that, all of a sudden,
she became a Sunday mass devotee.

792
01:04:37,360 --> 01:04:40,920
Your mother hadn't been well
for a long time, Ruth. Since you left.

793
01:04:42,720 --> 01:04:45,120
Sure. Now it's my fault, right?

794
01:04:45,200 --> 01:04:46,960
Do you know why I left, Dad?

795
01:04:47,040 --> 01:04:48,960
Because I couldn't take it anymore.

796
01:04:49,040 --> 01:04:51,800
Because I wanted to quit swimming,
and you wouldn't let me.

797
01:04:51,880 --> 01:04:53,600
And I wanted to live my life.

798
01:04:54,880 --> 01:04:56,760
Your mother adored you, Ruth.

799
01:04:57,560 --> 01:05:01,440
You were the light of her life and,
all of a sudden, you left her in the dark.

800
01:05:01,520 --> 01:05:03,800
Are you still surprised
that she took to drinking?

801
01:05:06,080 --> 01:05:09,600
Maybe she did because she couldn't stand
being next to a grumpy old man

802
01:05:09,680 --> 01:05:12,760
who was getting crazier every day
with his shitty conspiracies.

803
01:05:13,600 --> 01:05:16,880
Do you know how hard it is to love someone
who looks at you like that?

804
01:05:16,960 --> 01:05:19,760
Who doesn't respect you anymore?
Who doesn't love you anymore?

805
01:05:19,840 --> 01:05:22,400
Who suddenly only sees
a useless old man in you?

806
01:05:30,920 --> 01:05:34,320
I guess it's also my fault
that you're so sick in the head, right?

807
01:05:34,400 --> 01:05:38,480
I'm completely sane.
Sane as ever. I'm awake.

808
01:05:38,560 --> 01:05:41,800
It's you and the rest of you
who prefer to live in ignorance.

809
01:05:41,880 --> 01:05:44,720
Ignoring the suffering,
the pain of those kids and their families.

810
01:05:44,800 --> 01:05:48,920
I'm done. I can't deal with you going back
to your fucking insanity again and again.

811
01:05:49,000 --> 01:05:50,200
I'm not crazy!

812
01:05:50,280 --> 01:05:52,920
If there was an epidemic
of missing children,

813
01:05:53,000 --> 01:05:55,640
it would be on the front page
of every newspaper, dammit!

814
01:05:55,720 --> 01:05:57,520
You're so naive, honey.

815
01:05:57,600 --> 01:06:00,920
Do I have to explain to you at this point
how the world works?

816
01:06:02,320 --> 01:06:06,520
I'd rather think you're naive
and not something worse.

817
01:06:08,280 --> 01:06:10,920
Are you going to start suspecting me too?

818
01:06:11,000 --> 01:06:14,400
Sure, everyone is bribed except you.
Right, Dad?

819
01:06:14,480 --> 01:06:16,160
Everyone is an idiot except you.

820
01:06:16,240 --> 01:06:18,600
Have you looked at yourself in the mirror?

821
01:06:18,680 --> 01:06:20,240
Have you seen what you look like?

822
01:06:20,320 --> 01:06:22,880
Because it's not precisely
the look of a genius.

823
01:06:22,960 --> 01:06:24,800
You don't look like that enlightened man

824
01:06:24,880 --> 01:06:27,440
who knows the truth
and everyone has to follow.

825
01:06:28,040 --> 01:06:31,680
You're just a fucking old man locked up
in an attic doing God knows what.

826
01:06:31,760 --> 01:06:33,440
That's what you are, Dad.

827
01:06:34,520 --> 01:06:36,880
You want to know what I do in the attic,
don't you?

828
01:06:37,880 --> 01:06:40,640
Is that why you tried
to force the door yesterday?

829
01:06:42,960 --> 01:06:44,800
Have you been spying on me?

830
01:06:45,320 --> 01:06:47,160
- Great.
- Where are you going?

831
01:06:47,240 --> 01:06:49,640
- You'll get lost! Ruth!
- Let go of me!

832
01:06:51,840 --> 01:06:54,080
- Leave me alone!
- Ruth!

833
01:06:55,640 --> 01:06:56,600
Ruth.

834
01:06:57,600 --> 01:06:58,440
Ruth!

835
01:06:59,200 --> 01:07:00,040
Stop!

836
01:07:00,640 --> 01:07:01,720
Leave me alone!

837
01:07:09,000 --> 01:07:09,960
Ruth!

838
01:07:27,160 --> 01:07:30,160
You lost consciousness,
but you're fine now.

839
01:07:30,240 --> 01:07:34,040
You'll need a few days of rest,
or this ankle will suffer.

840
01:07:34,880 --> 01:07:37,080
If it hurts,
take a painkiller every eight hours.

841
01:07:37,160 --> 01:07:41,280
And drink the infusions your father makes.
He's good with herbs.

842
01:07:41,360 --> 01:07:45,360
But mainly, rest. We don't want
that ankle to heal badly, okay?

843
01:07:48,000 --> 01:07:49,000
Okay.

844
01:09:28,560 --> 01:09:31,520
What the fuck are all those cameras
doing around the house?

845
01:09:32,040 --> 01:09:34,680
Are you spying on me
or were you spying on Mom?

846
01:09:35,200 --> 01:09:38,200
What are you talking about?
Why would I spy on you? Don't be silly.

847
01:09:38,280 --> 01:09:39,760
Silly. I'm being silly now.

848
01:09:39,840 --> 01:09:41,960
I put them up for security.

849
01:09:42,040 --> 01:09:45,400
Because I was afraid
those people would come after us.

850
01:09:45,480 --> 01:09:46,520
For God's sake.

851
01:09:46,600 --> 01:09:50,680
You see? I knew you wouldn't believe me.
That's why I didn't tell you.

852
01:09:50,760 --> 01:09:52,800
Who is going to come after us, Dad?

853
01:09:52,880 --> 01:09:55,040
Who? The pharma company people?

854
01:09:55,120 --> 01:09:57,800
And the politicians. And the police.

855
01:09:57,880 --> 01:10:01,160
They can bribe anyone,
even the least expected.

856
01:10:01,920 --> 01:10:03,840
Think about it for a moment.

857
01:10:03,920 --> 01:10:06,680
They do those atrocities to kids.

858
01:10:06,760 --> 01:10:10,800
They're capable of doing anything
to any of us who bother them.

859
01:10:10,880 --> 01:10:12,760
And they know I'm investigating them.

860
01:10:12,840 --> 01:10:15,640
Not long ago, they caught me
snooping around their building.

861
01:10:15,720 --> 01:10:18,040
A guard asked me for my ID. I refused.

862
01:10:18,120 --> 01:10:20,760
But they know who I am and where I live.

863
01:10:24,400 --> 01:10:25,960
What were you doing there, Dad?

864
01:10:27,080 --> 01:10:29,440
The tunnels end there. In their basement.

865
01:10:29,520 --> 01:10:32,200
Look, I can't take it anymore. I can't.

866
01:10:32,280 --> 01:10:34,160
I can't stay here and keep a poker face

867
01:10:34,240 --> 01:10:36,520
while you tell me about tunnels
and kidnapped kids.

868
01:10:36,600 --> 01:10:37,640
I can't take this.

869
01:10:37,720 --> 01:10:40,560
You're not listening to me.
They're very dangerous.

870
01:10:40,640 --> 01:10:44,000
The only one following me
in her car is your friend.

871
01:10:44,080 --> 01:10:45,960
She's protecting you, Ruth.

872
01:10:47,480 --> 01:10:48,320
From what?

873
01:10:48,920 --> 01:10:51,440
She follows you to make sure you're okay.

874
01:10:51,520 --> 01:10:54,240
Being my daughter makes you a target.

875
01:11:00,960 --> 01:11:04,120
Were the cameras recording
the night Mom died?

876
01:11:05,640 --> 01:11:06,640
Of course.

877
01:11:08,040 --> 01:11:09,120
And what do they show?

878
01:11:09,720 --> 01:11:13,440
They show Mom going into the bathroom.
That's it.

879
01:11:15,160 --> 01:11:16,560
Does the Civil Guard know?

880
01:11:16,640 --> 01:11:18,600
Yes. I gave them everything.

881
01:11:18,680 --> 01:11:20,360
It was the first thing I did.

882
01:11:21,240 --> 01:11:23,200
Well, you didn't tell me.

883
01:11:23,280 --> 01:11:26,080
Because you wouldn't believe me.
That's why.

884
01:11:31,200 --> 01:11:33,000
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

885
01:11:34,480 --> 01:11:35,880
Let me show it to you, okay?

886
01:11:36,880 --> 01:11:40,000
This video is from when your mother
was already in the bathroom.

887
01:11:40,080 --> 01:11:42,680
The day... she died.

888
01:11:42,760 --> 01:11:43,760
That's right.

889
01:11:53,880 --> 01:11:55,160
But there's no sound.

890
01:11:55,240 --> 01:11:56,280
No, of course.

891
01:11:56,360 --> 01:11:59,000
We don't know if my mother answered.

892
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:01,840
What if my dad's just putting on a show?

893
01:12:01,920 --> 01:12:05,560
That would mean that your father
considered the cameras

894
01:12:05,640 --> 01:12:07,680
to execute a plan
that would give him an alibi

895
01:12:07,760 --> 01:12:10,080
and allowed him
to kill your mother in cold blood.

896
01:12:11,000 --> 01:12:12,200
Do you believe that?

897
01:12:13,040 --> 01:12:17,040
No. But my mother
locks herself in and falls.

898
01:12:17,120 --> 01:12:19,440
- How does she fall?
- The alcohol.

899
01:12:20,160 --> 01:12:22,800
My mother couldn't drink that much.

900
01:12:23,680 --> 01:12:24,680
Okay.

901
01:12:26,480 --> 01:12:28,880
This footage is from a few days earlier.

902
01:12:28,960 --> 01:12:31,040
It might shock you a little.

903
01:12:35,200 --> 01:12:38,120
She had clearly been drinking
that day too.

904
01:12:53,240 --> 01:12:56,720
- Please, turn it off.
- Yes. Of course. I'm sorry.

905
01:12:56,800 --> 01:13:00,680
Maybe I'm sticking my nose
where it doesn't belong.

906
01:13:02,440 --> 01:13:03,680
Is everything okay at home?

907
01:13:06,520 --> 01:13:08,440
We're here for whatever you need.

908
01:13:11,320 --> 01:13:12,320
Okay?

909
01:13:17,280 --> 01:13:20,520
How could you even think of driving
with your foot like that?

910
01:13:21,360 --> 01:13:22,960
Didn't you hear the doctor?

911
01:13:26,160 --> 01:13:28,360
Do you want to have an accident?

912
01:13:28,440 --> 01:13:29,680
Want me to lose you too?

913
01:13:30,280 --> 01:13:31,680
I needed to know.

914
01:13:32,440 --> 01:13:33,440
Know what?

915
01:13:34,280 --> 01:13:37,040
That your mother drank
until she passed out?

916
01:13:37,120 --> 01:13:38,840
Is that what you needed to know?

917
01:13:39,360 --> 01:13:42,280
That's exactly
what I wanted to protect you from, honey.

918
01:13:42,360 --> 01:13:43,880
From those images.

919
01:13:43,960 --> 01:13:45,520
You didn't need to know that.

920
01:13:45,600 --> 01:13:48,320
I don't want you
to remember your mother like that.

921
01:13:48,840 --> 01:13:49,720
I'm sorry.

922
01:13:51,080 --> 01:13:55,520
Promise me you'll rest
and stop overthinking.

923
01:13:56,320 --> 01:13:58,520
Take a shower and relax.

924
01:14:00,200 --> 01:14:02,440
Drink the herbs. They'll do you good.

925
01:15:03,240 --> 01:15:05,560
Ruth, do you need anything?

926
01:15:09,160 --> 01:15:11,160
Honey, are you okay?

927
01:15:12,280 --> 01:15:13,880
Ruth, say something.

928
01:15:16,960 --> 01:15:17,960
<i>Ruth?</i>

929
01:15:21,160 --> 01:15:22,880
<i>Are you okay? What's wrong?</i>

930
01:15:36,280 --> 01:15:39,760
It's so fucking weird. I was fine.

931
01:15:39,840 --> 01:15:43,200
Then I drank the fucking infusion
my dad gave me and started feeling dizzy.

932
01:15:43,280 --> 01:15:44,800
- You know?
- Okay.

933
01:15:44,880 --> 01:15:47,760
I began to lose my balance.

934
01:15:47,840 --> 01:15:49,960
Stumbling. Like my mother in those videos.

935
01:15:50,040 --> 01:15:53,640
So I started thinking
that maybe my dad gave her something.

936
01:15:53,720 --> 01:15:56,720
Something his friend, the coroner,
left out of the fucking report.

937
01:15:56,800 --> 01:15:58,320
- That's why it's not there.
- Wait.

938
01:15:58,400 --> 01:16:01,320
Hold on, because right now
I don't understand anything.

939
01:16:02,000 --> 01:16:03,640
And I'm a little high.

940
01:16:03,720 --> 01:16:08,160
I thought in the video you can see
your dad knocking and your mother not...

941
01:16:08,240 --> 01:16:09,560
Who told you that?

942
01:16:11,000 --> 01:16:13,440
Because you can see it,
but you can't hear anything.

943
01:16:13,520 --> 01:16:15,880
Maybe it was all a charade.

944
01:16:15,960 --> 01:16:17,840
Knocking on the door. Knocking it down.

945
01:16:17,920 --> 01:16:21,440
And when he entered and saw
that she couldn't move or anything,

946
01:16:21,520 --> 01:16:23,280
he killed her in cold blood, you know?

947
01:16:23,360 --> 01:16:26,760
Ruth, are you listening to yourself?
Do you hear what you're saying?

948
01:16:26,840 --> 01:16:30,560
He drugged me. I couldn't stand up.

949
01:16:30,640 --> 01:16:32,880
I don't know. Maybe you smoked.

950
01:16:32,960 --> 01:16:35,920
- I didn't smoke.
- Did you take something else?

951
01:16:36,000 --> 01:16:37,240
Nothing.

952
01:16:37,760 --> 01:16:38,920
I took...

953
01:16:39,000 --> 01:16:43,920
I took the painkiller for my foot
and whatever they gave me at the station.

954
01:16:44,000 --> 01:16:45,640
Well, maybe that's it.

955
01:16:47,680 --> 01:16:49,240
Why don't you believe me?

956
01:16:50,920 --> 01:16:53,280
What were you talking to my father about?

957
01:16:53,360 --> 01:16:54,880
I saw you two whispering.

958
01:16:54,960 --> 01:16:58,040
Ruth, your father is worried sick
about you. That's all.

959
01:16:58,120 --> 01:17:00,360
- Are you sure?
- Yes.

960
01:17:00,440 --> 01:17:01,800
Didn't he offer you a job?

961
01:17:02,760 --> 01:17:04,440
Look, that's enough.

962
01:17:04,520 --> 01:17:06,120
Okay, Ruth. That's it.

963
01:17:06,200 --> 01:17:09,840
Next time you think your dad's
trying to kill you or he's seeing aliens,

964
01:17:09,920 --> 01:17:11,320
call someone else, okay?

965
01:17:11,400 --> 01:17:13,000
Because I'm tired of it, Ruth.

966
01:17:13,080 --> 01:17:16,160
I'm tired of rushing over
every time you need a fuck,

967
01:17:16,240 --> 01:17:17,840
some weed, or a locksmith.

968
01:17:19,840 --> 01:17:20,840
Right.

969
01:17:22,400 --> 01:17:25,400
So I'm the one
taking advantage of you, right?

970
01:17:26,120 --> 01:17:29,880
Everything you do,
you do it selflessly. Right, Arón?

971
01:17:31,280 --> 01:17:34,960
Not because you think
maybe you and I can have something again.

972
01:17:37,160 --> 01:17:38,360
I'm done, Ruth.

973
01:18:28,400 --> 01:18:29,400
Ruth.

974
01:18:29,960 --> 01:18:31,280
Open the door.

975
01:18:32,000 --> 01:18:33,000
Ruth.

976
01:18:34,200 --> 01:18:35,800
Are you okay?

977
01:18:35,880 --> 01:18:37,920
Do you want me to call Dr. Velasco?

978
01:19:21,200 --> 01:19:23,200
FEBRUARY 26, 2024

979
01:19:27,720 --> 01:19:30,600
ALFONSO ALWAYS MAKES US SMILE

980
01:20:02,880 --> 01:20:04,680
CIVIL GUARD

981
01:20:24,960 --> 01:20:26,760
I'm here to talk to Sergeant Ballester.

982
01:20:32,240 --> 01:20:33,800
He's not answering. Please wait.

983
01:20:33,880 --> 01:20:36,160
- I can't wait. It's urgent.
- Hey!

984
01:20:41,720 --> 01:20:42,720
Hey!

985
01:20:52,280 --> 01:20:53,280
Excuse me.

986
01:20:54,720 --> 01:20:56,160
You can't be in here.

987
01:21:48,480 --> 01:21:50,880
Ruth! What are you doing?

988
01:21:52,520 --> 01:21:53,760
What are you doing?

989
01:21:58,560 --> 01:21:59,560
I'm sorry.

990
01:22:01,080 --> 01:22:02,680
I'm sorry, Dad.

991
01:22:07,000 --> 01:22:10,120
Can you make me
one of your infusions, please?

992
01:22:11,000 --> 01:22:12,000
Of course.

993
01:23:08,520 --> 01:23:12,400
UNKNOWN CALLER

994
01:23:12,480 --> 01:23:14,240
ACCEPT

995
01:23:17,160 --> 01:23:20,920
<i>Ruth, this is Sergeant Ballester.
They told me you came to see me.</i>

996
01:23:22,400 --> 01:23:23,600
Yes.

997
01:23:24,200 --> 01:23:27,160
<i>I guess you saw your father in my office.</i>

998
01:23:27,760 --> 01:23:29,160
<i>I think we need to talk.</i>

999
01:23:29,240 --> 01:23:30,360
Why?

1000
01:23:30,880 --> 01:23:34,480
<i>Your father came to see me this morning
to tell me that he thinks...</i>

1001
01:23:35,320 --> 01:23:40,360
<i>that somehow you could be involved
in your mother's death.</i>

1002
01:23:41,160 --> 01:23:43,960
<i>He thinks you're connected
to the pharmaceutical company</i>

1003
01:23:44,040 --> 01:23:49,040
<i>that set up a branch in the area,
and might be working with other people.</i>

1004
01:23:49,920 --> 01:23:52,200
<i>That there's no such thing as coincidence.</i>

1005
01:23:52,280 --> 01:23:56,240
<i>Anyway. The ramblings of someone
who's lonely and has too much free time.</i>

1006
01:23:56,960 --> 01:23:58,760
<i>We've been seeing this a lot lately.</i>

1007
01:23:59,600 --> 01:24:00,720
MISSING BOY
CHECK LAKE

1008
01:24:00,800 --> 01:24:02,240
ARE THEY WORKING FOR THEM?

1009
01:24:02,320 --> 01:24:06,960
<i>I just want you to know that for us,
and for anyone with half a brain,</i>

1010
01:24:07,040 --> 01:24:09,840
<i>the case is closed and there was no crime.</i>

1011
01:24:11,480 --> 01:24:12,880
It was an accident.

1012
01:24:13,400 --> 01:24:17,600
<i>Each and every piece of evidence points
to it being an unfortunate accident, yes.</i>

1013
01:24:23,240 --> 01:24:24,720
Then why?

1014
01:24:25,400 --> 01:24:26,400
<i>Why what?</i>

1015
01:24:30,400 --> 01:24:33,240
Why make up all those things?

1016
01:24:35,040 --> 01:24:40,400
<i>Because I guess it's very hard to accept
that you've lost the person you love...</i>

1017
01:24:40,480 --> 01:24:42,160
<i>in such a silly way.</i>

1018
01:24:49,720 --> 01:24:52,120
It's always easier to blame someone else.

1019
01:24:55,200 --> 01:24:57,720
<i>It's not easy to know
what to do with so much pain.</i>

1020
01:24:58,400 --> 01:25:03,320
<i>I think those ideas
make your father feel special, useful.</i>

1021
01:25:11,000 --> 01:25:16,240
<i>But I just wanted to warn you.
He has the conviction of a fanatic.</i>

1022
01:25:16,320 --> 01:25:18,080
<i>And a fanatic is...</i>

1023
01:25:19,080 --> 01:25:20,880
<i>the most dangerous kind of person.</i>

1024
01:25:30,360 --> 01:25:32,160
<i>Be careful with him, please.</i>

1025
01:25:34,320 --> 01:25:35,160
Thank you.

1026
01:25:49,040 --> 01:25:50,040
Dad?

1027
01:25:53,560 --> 01:25:54,520
Dad.

1028
01:25:55,280 --> 01:25:56,120
I'm sorry.

1029
01:25:56,200 --> 01:25:58,560
- Hey. Stay away from him.
- I'm sorry.

1030
01:25:58,640 --> 01:25:59,720
I just want to talk.

1031
01:25:59,800 --> 01:26:01,920
You almost killed him, dammit.

1032
01:26:02,000 --> 01:26:04,800
Let me explain. Everything's fine.

1033
01:26:05,920 --> 01:26:06,920
Dad.

1034
01:26:07,560 --> 01:26:09,160
He's afraid of you. Can't you see?

1035
01:26:09,240 --> 01:26:10,680
And no wonder.

1036
01:26:12,200 --> 01:26:14,160
- Damn it, Dad.
- Give me your hands.

1037
01:26:15,960 --> 01:26:18,800
- If you want to talk, it'll be tied up.
- What?

1038
01:26:18,880 --> 01:26:21,800
We can't risk you attacking him again.

1039
01:26:21,880 --> 01:26:23,200
Dad.

1040
01:26:28,520 --> 01:26:30,080
Do as she says, honey.

1041
01:26:44,240 --> 01:26:47,320
I thought you had something to do
with Mom's death.

1042
01:26:48,520 --> 01:26:50,640
I'm sorry. I'm sorry, Dad.

1043
01:26:50,720 --> 01:26:52,640
I didn't realize. I...

1044
01:26:52,720 --> 01:26:54,600
I didn't want to hurt you, really.

1045
01:26:57,880 --> 01:27:00,000
What are you going to do?

1046
01:27:00,080 --> 01:27:02,360
Are you going to call the Civil Guard?

1047
01:27:04,200 --> 01:27:05,200
What is it?

1048
01:27:07,120 --> 01:27:09,920
- Fuck!
- This is not necessary.

1049
01:27:13,080 --> 01:27:14,040
Come here.

1050
01:27:19,760 --> 01:27:22,840
Right now we have a problem
with your daughter, Martín.

1051
01:27:22,920 --> 01:27:25,520
We don't know what she's seen
or hasn't seen in the attic.

1052
01:27:28,080 --> 01:27:29,360
We have to act now.

1053
01:27:30,240 --> 01:27:33,760
We have to get to the tunnels.
We need to find the children.

1054
01:27:41,200 --> 01:27:42,560
Who the hell is it now?

1055
01:27:42,640 --> 01:27:45,160
It's the doctor.
He must have come to see Ruth.

1056
01:27:46,080 --> 01:27:47,520
Tell him to leave.

1057
01:27:47,600 --> 01:27:48,920
What's that, Dad?

1058
01:27:49,520 --> 01:27:50,960
Is that Arón's badge?

1059
01:27:55,520 --> 01:27:57,680
Dad! No! Dad!

1060
01:27:57,760 --> 01:27:59,480
Dad, please, don't leave me here!

1061
01:27:59,560 --> 01:28:00,600
Shut up, dammit.

1062
01:28:00,680 --> 01:28:02,240
- Dad!
- <i>Let's go with the words...</i>

1063
01:28:02,320 --> 01:28:03,560
Help!

1064
01:28:03,640 --> 01:28:05,080
Will you shut up?

1065
01:28:05,600 --> 01:28:07,080
- Dad!
- You will shut up.

1066
01:28:07,160 --> 01:28:12,720
<i>...in Asturian.
And today's nice word has six letters.</i>

1067
01:28:13,760 --> 01:28:15,800
<i>Inma? No?</i>

1068
01:28:15,880 --> 01:28:18,400
Let's calm down a bit.

1069
01:28:21,200 --> 01:28:24,200
Hey, calm down. I'll take care of it.

1070
01:28:24,280 --> 01:28:27,160
<i>That nice word doesn't smell very good.</i>

1071
01:28:28,360 --> 01:28:30,160
I'll wait for you in the car.

1072
01:28:31,680 --> 01:28:33,360
<i>No. Rebound.</i>

1073
01:28:46,000 --> 01:28:48,640
Dad, what are you going to do
with Arón's badge?

1074
01:28:50,240 --> 01:28:52,280
Is that what Mom saw?
What you're about to do?

1075
01:28:52,880 --> 01:28:55,520
Is that why you deleted
everything on her phone?

1076
01:28:55,600 --> 01:28:57,400
She went into the attic too.

1077
01:28:57,480 --> 01:29:01,720
She found out what we're going to do
and she went nuts.

1078
01:29:01,800 --> 01:29:04,400
And then, yes,
she started drinking more than usual.

1079
01:29:04,480 --> 01:29:07,600
But I don't think it was an accident.

1080
01:29:07,680 --> 01:29:09,480
I think someone killed her.

1081
01:29:10,920 --> 01:29:13,160
Do you really think
I had something to do with it?

1082
01:29:13,240 --> 01:29:16,240
I know you'd never
do anything to your mother.

1083
01:29:16,320 --> 01:29:21,440
But maybe they're using you to try
to implicate me and get me out of the way.

1084
01:29:21,520 --> 01:29:24,240
I didn't think so at first, but all this,

1085
01:29:24,320 --> 01:29:29,760
your mother's death just after they saw me
wandering around their building,

1086
01:29:29,840 --> 01:29:31,320
is too much of a coincidence.

1087
01:29:32,120 --> 01:29:33,080
Mom fell.

1088
01:29:33,800 --> 01:29:36,440
I know it's hard to accept,
but we have no choice.

1089
01:29:36,520 --> 01:29:40,000
I know it's terrifying,
but making up stories doesn't help.

1090
01:29:40,080 --> 01:29:43,160
I told the doctor you were sleeping
and to come by this afternoon.

1091
01:29:43,240 --> 01:29:44,640
He'll untie you.

1092
01:29:49,320 --> 01:29:50,320
Dad.

1093
01:29:51,440 --> 01:29:52,280
Dad!

1094
01:29:52,880 --> 01:29:53,880
Fuck.

1095
01:30:11,720 --> 01:30:12,720
Fuck.

1096
01:30:18,080 --> 01:30:19,400
Help!

1097
01:30:19,920 --> 01:30:21,480
Help me, please!

1098
01:30:29,880 --> 01:30:33,040
SEARCH CONTINUES FOR ALEJANDRO HUELVA

1099
01:30:40,680 --> 01:30:41,880
He's in the lake.

1100
01:31:35,840 --> 01:31:37,760
Arón, please, listen to me.

1101
01:31:38,360 --> 01:31:41,360
<i>I know I've said a lot of nonsense lately.</i>

1102
01:31:41,440 --> 01:31:43,920
<i>I know. But this isn't.</i>

1103
01:31:44,000 --> 01:31:47,880
<i>My father has your badge
and he'll use it to get into the building.</i>

1104
01:31:48,680 --> 01:31:49,880
<i>He may already be inside.</i>

1105
01:31:49,960 --> 01:31:52,880
<i>And that's not all.
He has a gun and explosives.</i>

1106
01:31:52,960 --> 01:31:57,040
<i>The woman who's with him
was in the army and knows how to use them.</i>

1107
01:31:57,120 --> 01:31:58,400
<i>They're headed to the basement.</i>

1108
01:31:58,480 --> 01:32:03,280
<i>They think there are children hidden
in secret tunnels or something.</i>

1109
01:32:03,360 --> 01:32:05,800
<i>God knows what they're capable of doing
to find them.</i>

1110
01:32:05,880 --> 01:32:09,360
<i>I'm on my way.
But I might not get there in time.</i>

1111
01:32:09,440 --> 01:32:13,000
<i>I need you to believe me.
Please, Arón. Please.</i>

1112
01:32:14,680 --> 01:32:17,080
...<i>and he'll use it
to get into the building.</i>

1113
01:32:17,160 --> 01:32:19,080
<i>He may already be inside.</i>

1114
01:32:19,160 --> 01:32:22,000
<i>And that's not all.
He has a gun and explosives.</i>

1115
01:32:24,360 --> 01:32:25,360
Careful.

1116
01:32:37,360 --> 01:32:38,640
Wait.

1117
01:32:41,000 --> 01:32:42,000
Let's go.

1118
01:32:52,880 --> 01:32:53,960
I'll go.

1119
01:33:25,520 --> 01:33:26,480
All good.

1120
01:33:28,400 --> 01:33:29,400
Let's do it.

1121
01:33:53,680 --> 01:33:55,200
Stop. You can't go in.

1122
01:33:55,280 --> 01:33:57,680
My father is inside. I have to go in.

1123
01:34:16,040 --> 01:34:18,680
Arón. Hey, are you okay?

1124
01:34:28,320 --> 01:34:31,840
Let's try the next wall.
They must be behind it.

1125
01:34:41,400 --> 01:34:42,440
Dad.

1126
01:34:43,120 --> 01:34:45,320
Ruth! What are you doing here? Go home.

1127
01:34:45,400 --> 01:34:47,000
No. You're coming with me.

1128
01:34:47,760 --> 01:34:48,880
I can't, honey.

1129
01:34:48,960 --> 01:34:50,200
The boy is not here.

1130
01:34:50,280 --> 01:34:52,440
Remember I cut myself
on some metal at the lake?

1131
01:34:52,520 --> 01:34:54,760
It was Alejandro's bike.

1132
01:34:54,840 --> 01:34:58,040
The boy is there.
He's drowned with the bike. I'm sure.

1133
01:34:58,920 --> 01:35:01,880
You'd say anything
to make me change my mind.

1134
01:35:01,960 --> 01:35:04,720
No, Dad. If you don't believe me,
we can go to the police.

1135
01:35:04,800 --> 01:35:06,800
I'm sure they'll find him.
He's in the lake.

1136
01:35:06,880 --> 01:35:10,680
Even if that were true,
it's not just Alejandro.

1137
01:35:10,760 --> 01:35:12,680
It's all the other children.

1138
01:35:12,760 --> 01:35:15,120
Those who were,
and those who'd come after.

1139
01:35:15,200 --> 01:35:18,320
Don't you see that,
by uncovering the truth, we can save them?

1140
01:35:18,400 --> 01:35:21,920
I understand you, really.
But you don't have to save anyone.

1141
01:35:22,000 --> 01:35:23,760
You don't have to save any children.

1142
01:35:23,840 --> 01:35:27,000
You don't have to change the world.
You don't have to be a hero.

1143
01:35:28,280 --> 01:35:30,920
Isn't it enough for you to be my father?

1144
01:35:31,000 --> 01:35:32,480
Because I do need you.

1145
01:35:34,000 --> 01:35:36,960
Let's go. Let's think of another plan.
Let's come back another day.

1146
01:35:37,040 --> 01:35:39,240
Whatever you want.
I'm not leaving you anymore.

1147
01:35:39,320 --> 01:35:41,280
I'll stay right by your side. I swear.

1148
01:35:41,360 --> 01:35:42,280
I swear, Dad.

1149
01:35:44,400 --> 01:35:45,560
I love you.

1150
01:35:50,560 --> 01:35:53,800
- No! Dad!
- Let's go! Ruth!

1151
01:36:44,960 --> 01:36:47,880
<i>Today we woke up
to the sad news that Alejandro Huelva,</i>

1152
01:36:47,960 --> 01:36:49,600
<i>the boy who disappeared a month ago,</i>

1153
01:36:49,680 --> 01:36:51,880
<i>has been found dead in Lake Caldas.</i>

1154
01:36:51,960 --> 01:36:55,120
<i>Alejandro's body
was found trapped in some debris.</i>

1155
01:36:55,200 --> 01:36:57,960
<i>A tragedy that adds
to the deaths last week</i>

1156
01:36:58,040 --> 01:36:59,720
<i>of Martín Arce and Berta Martínez,</i>

1157
01:36:59,800 --> 01:37:02,520
<i>victims of the explosions
caused by themselves</i>

1158
01:37:02,600 --> 01:37:05,000
<i>in the basement
of the pharmaceutical company Certyx.</i>

1159
01:37:05,080 --> 01:37:07,400
<i>Apparently, both believed
that in the basement</i>

1160
01:37:07,480 --> 01:37:12,760
<i>there were tunnels where they would find
Alejandro and other missing children.</i>

1161
01:37:12,840 --> 01:37:14,440
<i>A conspiracy theory that,</i>

1162
01:37:14,520 --> 01:37:17,520
<i>in recent months,
has been gaining a lot of traction--</i>

1163
01:37:43,320 --> 01:37:45,400
ONE YEAR LATER

1164
01:38:45,040 --> 01:38:46,040
Hello.

1165
01:38:46,560 --> 01:38:48,520
You're Martín's daughter, right?

1166
01:38:50,840 --> 01:38:52,600
The one from the pharma company.

1167
01:38:54,440 --> 01:38:56,840
Tell me something. He's not dead, is he?

1168
01:38:57,600 --> 01:38:58,560
What?

1169
01:38:59,280 --> 01:39:02,000
It's all a cover
so he can keep investigating, right?

1170
01:39:02,080 --> 01:39:03,760
So they'll leave him alone.

1171
01:39:04,680 --> 01:39:05,960
No one can know.

1172
01:39:06,040 --> 01:39:09,480
Because the police and the media
are all in on it.

1173
01:39:09,560 --> 01:39:13,040
It's clear they experimented on that boy
and then threw him in the lake.

1174
01:39:14,600 --> 01:39:16,160
What are you talking about?

1175
01:39:17,040 --> 01:39:19,120
Don't you realize
that doesn't make any sense?

1176
01:39:23,400 --> 01:39:24,400
Right.

1177
01:39:25,200 --> 01:39:28,280
You can't talk, right?
Does it put you in danger?

1178
01:39:29,320 --> 01:39:30,680
I get it.

1179
01:39:30,760 --> 01:39:32,920
But one day the truth will come out.

1180
01:39:33,000 --> 01:39:35,600
I think your father
will bring everything to light.

1181
01:39:35,680 --> 01:39:38,720
He's an inspiration for so many people.

1182
01:39:39,240 --> 01:39:40,680
Tell him that from me.

1183
01:39:43,440 --> 01:39:44,440
Cool.

1184
01:40:17,920 --> 01:40:24,920
NO MORE LIES

1185
01:40:26,960 --> 01:40:30,000
{\an8}FREE OUR CHILDREN NOW

1186
01:40:30,080 --> 01:40:32,920
{\an8}WE WANT THE TRUTH

1187
01:40:33,000 --> 01:40:36,880
{\an8}NO MORE LIES. WAKE UP!

1188
01:44:19,040 --> 01:44:24,040
Subtitle translation by: Olga Garrido



